WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.212 --> 00:00:05.880
- Where are we?

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:07.090
- So this is our studio,

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.842
where we shoot our sitcoms

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.260
and our game shows

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:11.344
and our court shows.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:13.304
It's something that's

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:15.015
really durable and flexible,

00:00:15.015 --> 00:00:16.182
and we're able to swap,

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:17.434
you know, sets in and out.

00:00:17.434 --> 00:00:19.060
So this is "Mr. Box Office."

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.396
Over here we have our comedy game show,

00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:23.106
"Funny You Should Ask."

00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:24.190
- I'm gonna have to sit over here

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:25.066
one of these days.

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:26.026
- Yeah, we have great people

00:00:26.026 --> 00:00:27.193
coming through -

00:00:27.193 --> 00:00:30.697
Jon Lovitz and Sherri Shepherd and...

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.449
- Hello, Sherri?

00:00:32.449 --> 00:00:35.118
(Audience laughing)

00:00:35.118 --> 00:00:36.494
- It's great, I mean, you know,

00:00:36.494 --> 00:00:38.121
you just want to have a place where

00:00:38.121 --> 00:00:39.622
when you have ideas

00:00:39.622 --> 00:00:41.374
you can immediately put 'em up.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:42.834
I mean, this is our factory.

00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:45.003
This is our content factory.

00:00:45.003 --> 00:00:47.130
And, you know, Henry Ford,

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:47.964
a 100 years ago,

00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:48.798
he's got a factory,

00:00:48.798 --> 00:00:50.925
and he's coming out with his Model T

00:00:50.925 --> 00:00:52.510
or he is coming out with his Mustang.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:53.970
Same thing, so...

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.680
- Everything in house - you control

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.307
what it looks like.

00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:58.141
- That's right.

00:00:58.141 --> 00:00:58.975
- Everything.

00:00:58.975 --> 00:00:59.851
- Vertical integration.

00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:02.187
That's what you want,
you know, from A to Z.

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:10.153
- Yo, what's happening?

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:10.987
- How you doing, man?

00:01:10.987 --> 00:01:12.822
- I'm well, I'm well, good to see you.

00:01:12.822 --> 00:01:13.656
- Good to see you.

00:01:13.656 --> 00:01:15.033
- So first and foremost,

00:01:15.033 --> 00:01:16.159
I wanna start out

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:17.452
by talking about your journey.

00:01:17.452 --> 00:01:18.453
So if you don't mind,

00:01:18.453 --> 00:01:20.413
if you would introduce yourself.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:21.664
- I am Byron Allen.

00:01:21.664 --> 00:01:23.792
I was born April 22nd, 1961,

00:01:23.792 --> 00:01:25.710
in Detroit, Michigan -

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.670
the great Detroit, Michigan.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.380
My mother got pregnant with me

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.633
when she was 16 years old.

00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:32.467
- Wow.

00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:34.594
- A little Black teenage girl

00:01:34.594 --> 00:01:36.888
in Detroit, Michigan, in 1961,

00:01:36.888 --> 00:01:38.473
having this little Black baby,

00:01:38.473 --> 00:01:40.058
you probably wouldn't expect much.

00:01:40.058 --> 00:01:41.768
But thank God we're in America.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:42.811
And I was really fortunate.

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:45.355
My mother is not only beautiful,

00:01:45.355 --> 00:01:46.606
she's brilliant.

00:01:46.606 --> 00:01:49.442
And we came out here to Los Angeles,

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:52.237
and we ended up staying.

00:01:52.237 --> 00:01:54.114
And we just, for a couple years,

00:01:54.114 --> 00:01:55.532
slept on a lot of sofas,

00:01:55.532 --> 00:01:56.991
a lot of floors,

00:01:56.991 --> 00:01:59.035
slept wherever we could at friends'.

00:01:59.035 --> 00:01:59.869
And my mother ended up

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:02.705
getting into college at UCLA

00:02:02.705 --> 00:02:05.250
and going to film and TV school.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.710
And she ended up getting her master's

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.504
in cinema TV production,

00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:12.006
which is why education's everything.

00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:13.925
And she couldn't afford child care.

00:02:13.925 --> 00:02:16.344
So I used to go to class with my mother,

00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:17.679
and I would sit there in class

00:02:17.679 --> 00:02:19.764
waiting for her to get outta school.

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:20.598
And then I would go

00:02:20.598 --> 00:02:21.975
and watch her edit for her

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:23.810
film and TV classes.

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:27.814
And she one day went to NBC

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:29.357
and said "Can I have a job?"

00:02:29.357 --> 00:02:31.276
And they said, "We don't have a job here."

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:33.111
And then she said,

00:02:33.111 --> 00:02:34.988
"Do you have an intern program,

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:37.157
where somebody can work here for free

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:38.658
so they can learn?"

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:41.286
And they said, "No we don't have that."

00:02:41.286 --> 00:02:42.579
And then she asked a question

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:44.289
that changed our lives forever.

00:02:44.289 --> 00:02:47.625
She said, "Will you start one with me?"

00:02:48.585 --> 00:02:51.004
And they said yes. - That's powerful.

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:52.380
- And my mother got a job

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.549
working at NBC for free.

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:56.009
And she couldn't afford child care.

00:02:56.009 --> 00:02:56.843
She needed to take

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:58.219
this little Black baby with her.

00:02:58.219 --> 00:02:59.053
I'm sitting there

00:02:59.053 --> 00:02:59.888
waiting for her to get off work.

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:01.806
And I'm watching Johnny Carson

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:03.099
do "The Tonight Show."

00:03:03.099 --> 00:03:04.851
And then I'm walking across the hall,

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:05.685
and I'm watching

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:07.312
Redd Foxx do "Sanford and Son."

00:03:07.312 --> 00:03:08.271
Then I go across the hall,

00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:09.147
and I watch Flip Wilson

00:03:09.147 --> 00:03:10.064
do "The Flip Wilson Show."

00:03:10.064 --> 00:03:11.482
Then I go across the hall again,

00:03:11.482 --> 00:03:12.358
I watch Freddie Prinze

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:13.193
do "Chico and the Man."

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:14.027
- You're seeing all of
this at a young age.

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:15.236
- I'm just going from studio

00:03:15.236 --> 00:03:16.571
to studio to studio.

00:03:16.571 --> 00:03:18.156
I'm watching 'em do talk shows,

00:03:18.156 --> 00:03:20.700
game shows, soap operas, and sitcoms.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.535
I said, "You know what?

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:24.746
This is how I'm gonna spend my life,

00:03:24.746 --> 00:03:26.122
making television

00:03:26.122 --> 00:03:28.166
and making people laugh."

00:03:28.166 --> 00:03:29.209
- So is that when it hit you?

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:30.168
- That's when it hit me.

00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:31.002
- That's when it hit you?

00:03:31.002 --> 00:03:31.836
- 12 years old,

00:03:31.836 --> 00:03:33.755
and I'm sitting there
and I'm just going...

00:03:33.755 --> 00:03:35.256
And it was so cool, because

00:03:35.256 --> 00:03:38.176
I would stay - I would
stand in the parking lot

00:03:38.176 --> 00:03:40.094
every day at like around two o'clock,

00:03:40.094 --> 00:03:41.095
because Johnny Carson

00:03:41.095 --> 00:03:42.931
would pull into his parking space

00:03:42.931 --> 00:03:45.516
like clockwork at two o'clock.

00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:46.643
You look up - Bam!

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:48.603
Johnny Carson pullin' into his spot

00:03:48.603 --> 00:03:50.146
and I say, "Hello Mr. Carson.

00:03:50.146 --> 00:03:51.272
How you doing Mr. Car...

00:03:51.272 --> 00:03:52.774
Your monologue yester - last night

00:03:52.774 --> 00:03:53.608
was great.

00:03:53.608 --> 00:03:54.901
I love that joke about so-and-so."

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.152
And he said, "Thank you, thank you."

00:03:56.152 --> 00:03:57.070
and over time he's like,

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:57.904
"Thank you Byron."

00:03:57.904 --> 00:03:58.738
He got to know my name.

00:03:58.738 --> 00:03:59.572
- He knew your name.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:01.824
- Because I would stay - I
would conveniently be there

00:04:01.824 --> 00:04:02.742
by that parking space,

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.160
"Mr. Carson, that joke" - Right?

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:05.578
I would always do that.

00:04:05.578 --> 00:04:07.205
And Redd Foxx talked to me

00:04:07.205 --> 00:04:08.998
like I was his best buddy.

00:04:08.998 --> 00:04:10.166
Like I'm like a little...

00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:11.584
- I would've absolutely loved

00:04:11.584 --> 00:04:12.418
to meet Redd Foxx.

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:13.253
- Oh, my God, man!

00:04:13.253 --> 00:04:14.087
He talked to me.

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.171
He would come...

00:04:15.171 --> 00:04:16.005
He was old-school.

00:04:16.005 --> 00:04:16.839
So he'd come up to me, Chris,

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:17.674
he'd be like, "Hey Byron,

00:04:17.674 --> 00:04:18.508
come here, come here."

00:04:18.508 --> 00:04:19.676
He'd go, "You want some cabbage?

00:04:19.676 --> 00:04:20.802
You want some cabbage?"

00:04:20.802 --> 00:04:21.636
And I'd go, "Cabbage?"

00:04:21.636 --> 00:04:24.055
And he pulled out a wad of cash,

00:04:24.055 --> 00:04:25.223
a wad of cash.

00:04:25.223 --> 00:04:26.057
He'd go, "Look at that cabbage,

00:04:26.057 --> 00:04:26.891
look at that cabbage."

00:04:26.891 --> 00:04:27.725
(Byron laughing)

00:04:27.725 --> 00:04:28.559
- Right see,

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:29.394
so my question to you

00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:30.228
is at 12 years old, right?

00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:31.646
Like when I was a young kid,

00:04:31.646 --> 00:04:33.648
I watched everything Michael Jordan did.

00:04:33.648 --> 00:04:34.482
- Yeah.

00:04:34.482 --> 00:04:35.316
- I watched every game

00:04:35.316 --> 00:04:36.276
that came on WGN,

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:38.528
and just like a lot of kids,

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:39.362
it's a dream.

00:04:39.362 --> 00:04:40.196
- That's right.

00:04:40.196 --> 00:04:41.197
- It's a dream.

00:04:41.197 --> 00:04:42.657
So for you running into them,

00:04:42.657 --> 00:04:43.574
you know, that was a dream,

00:04:43.574 --> 00:04:45.118
and you hoped and you looked up to them.

00:04:45.118 --> 00:04:46.953
But how did you start your career?

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:48.997
Like when did you start your career?

00:04:48.997 --> 00:04:49.956
Like after 12 -

00:04:49.956 --> 00:04:51.082
when did you say this is...

00:04:51.082 --> 00:04:52.000
- This is it?

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:52.834
- This is what I'ma do.

00:04:52.834 --> 00:04:53.751
- Yeah, you know,

00:04:53.751 --> 00:04:54.752
Gladys Knight &amp; the Pips

00:04:54.752 --> 00:04:56.004
had a summer show,

00:04:56.004 --> 00:04:57.505
and they taped it at NBC.

00:04:57.505 --> 00:05:00.717
Summer of '75, I was 14 years old.

00:05:01.968 --> 00:05:03.052
And they had a comedian on -

00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:04.012
it was Gabe Kaplan.

00:05:05.138 --> 00:05:05.972
And I went and knocked

00:05:05.972 --> 00:05:07.265
on his dressing room door,

00:05:07.265 --> 00:05:08.558
and I said,

00:05:08.558 --> 00:05:09.642
"Wow, sir, you know,

00:05:09.642 --> 00:05:12.061
I wanna be a comedian like you.

00:05:12.061 --> 00:05:13.354
What do I do?"

00:05:13.354 --> 00:05:14.939
He said, "Go to the Comedy Store."

00:05:14.939 --> 00:05:15.940
I called the Comedy Store,

00:05:15.940 --> 00:05:17.025
I go, "Is this the Comedy Store?"

00:05:17.025 --> 00:05:17.942
They go, "Yeah."

00:05:17.942 --> 00:05:19.694
And I said, "So let me ask you something.

00:05:19.694 --> 00:05:21.446
Do you like, do you sell jokes?

00:05:21.446 --> 00:05:22.697
Or do you sell gags?"

00:05:22.697 --> 00:05:23.698
They go,

00:05:23.698 --> 00:05:24.532
"What the hell are you talking about?

00:05:24.532 --> 00:05:25.366
This is not a supermarket
- it's a comedy club.

00:05:25.366 --> 00:05:26.284
It's called..."

00:05:26.284 --> 00:05:27.201
I go "OK."

00:05:27.201 --> 00:05:28.119
I go, "I wanna be a comedian."

00:05:28.119 --> 00:05:28.953
They go, "OK,

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:30.121
we have Monday night tryout nights."

00:05:30.121 --> 00:05:31.122
"Monday night tryout nights?"

00:05:31.122 --> 00:05:31.956
They go, "Yeah."

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:32.957
They said, "You know,

00:05:32.957 --> 00:05:34.083
you can go onstage

00:05:34.083 --> 00:05:35.126
at like eight o'clock,

00:05:35.126 --> 00:05:36.753
but you gotta get here and sign up.

00:05:36.753 --> 00:05:38.338
But you should get here early."

00:05:38.338 --> 00:05:39.213
I go, "OK, no problem.

00:05:39.213 --> 00:05:40.256
I'll get there early."

00:05:40.256 --> 00:05:42.216
He didn't know I was 14 years old.

00:05:42.216 --> 00:05:44.135
I get there at nine in the morning.

00:05:44.135 --> 00:05:46.179
So I get there at nine in the morning,

00:05:46.179 --> 00:05:48.139
and I sit on the curb.

00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:49.974
I literally sat on the curb

00:05:49.974 --> 00:05:52.143
from nine in the morning - on Sunset -

00:05:52.143 --> 00:05:53.353
until like 7:30 at night,

00:05:53.353 --> 00:05:54.771
and I'm just writin' jokes.

00:05:54.771 --> 00:05:55.605
Club opens, Mitzi Shore,

00:05:55.605 --> 00:05:56.898
God bless her.

00:05:56.898 --> 00:05:59.317
So she opens the door,

00:05:59.317 --> 00:06:00.943
and I'm thinking, Oh, my God,

00:06:00.943 --> 00:06:01.903
I hope she doesn't look up.

00:06:01.903 --> 00:06:03.446
She's just doing her thing.

00:06:03.446 --> 00:06:04.906
But my voice gave it away.

00:06:04.906 --> 00:06:06.449
She goes, "OK, what's your name?"

00:06:06.449 --> 00:06:07.992
I go, "Byron."

00:06:07.992 --> 00:06:09.369
And she looks up,

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:11.704
and she goes, "How old are you?"

00:06:11.704 --> 00:06:13.331
I go, "I'm 14, ma'am."

00:06:13.331 --> 00:06:14.791
She goes, "You can't be in here.

00:06:14.791 --> 00:06:16.542
I'll lose my liquor license."

00:06:16.542 --> 00:06:18.169
She goes, and I started to walk.

00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:19.003
She goes, "Wait a minute."

00:06:19.003 --> 00:06:19.921
She goes,

00:06:19.921 --> 00:06:21.923
"You just stay in the parking lot.

00:06:21.923 --> 00:06:22.757
And when it's time

00:06:22.757 --> 00:06:23.591
for you to go onstage,

00:06:23.591 --> 00:06:24.801
I'll send somebody to get you.

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:25.802
But you go onstage,

00:06:25.802 --> 00:06:26.886
and you immediately get outta here."

00:06:26.886 --> 00:06:27.720
- Get outta here.

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:30.056
- So I go onstage, I do my thing.

00:06:30.056 --> 00:06:33.184
And this guy comes up to me, and he goes,

00:06:33.184 --> 00:06:34.894
"Who wrote those jokes?"

00:06:34.894 --> 00:06:36.312
I go, "I wrote those jokes."

00:06:36.312 --> 00:06:37.146
He goes, "How old are you?"

00:06:37.146 --> 00:06:38.314
I say, "I'm 14."

00:06:38.314 --> 00:06:40.191
He goes, "Can I get your phone number?"

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:41.526
I go, "Sure."

00:06:41.526 --> 00:06:42.527
He goes, "I may have a friend

00:06:42.527 --> 00:06:44.529
who wants to write jokes with you.

00:06:44.529 --> 00:06:45.947
I got - we write jokes with some people."

00:06:45.947 --> 00:06:46.781
I said, "OK."

00:06:46.781 --> 00:06:47.740
I give him my number.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.534
I get a call one week later,

00:06:49.534 --> 00:06:50.493
and this guy calls me up.

00:06:50.493 --> 00:06:52.578
He goes, "Can I speak to Byron?"

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:53.913
I go, "Yeah this is Byron."

00:06:53.913 --> 00:06:57.875
He goes, "This is Jimmie J.J. Walker,

00:06:57.875 --> 00:06:58.876
from 'Good Times.'"

00:06:58.876 --> 00:06:59.710
- Yes, exactly.

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:00.837
- He's hotter than the sun.

00:07:00.837 --> 00:07:03.047
This is 1975, right?

00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:04.173
And he says,

00:07:04.173 --> 00:07:06.884
"My man, Wayne Klein,

00:07:06.884 --> 00:07:08.636
says you're funny.

00:07:08.636 --> 00:07:10.638
And if my man Wayne Klein

00:07:10.638 --> 00:07:11.973
says you're funny,

00:07:11.973 --> 00:07:13.433
then you're funny."

00:07:13.433 --> 00:07:14.892
He says, "So listen up.

00:07:14.892 --> 00:07:16.519
Why don't you come on over here

00:07:16.519 --> 00:07:17.728
and write some jokes

00:07:17.728 --> 00:07:19.105
with me and my guys.

00:07:19.105 --> 00:07:20.106
You wanna write some jokes

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:21.524
with me and my guys?"

00:07:21.524 --> 00:07:24.068
And I go, "Let me ask my mom."

00:07:24.068 --> 00:07:25.862
And so, he goes, "Oh, my God,

00:07:25.862 --> 00:07:27.488
he's gotta ask his mom."

00:07:27.488 --> 00:07:28.322
- Cause you're kid.

00:07:28.322 --> 00:07:29.282
- I'm kid, right.

00:07:29.282 --> 00:07:30.324
So then I heard this dude

00:07:30.324 --> 00:07:31.200
in the background goes,

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:32.493
"Tell his mom not to worry.

00:07:32.493 --> 00:07:33.327
We'll have some cookies

00:07:33.327 --> 00:07:34.662
and milk for him."

00:07:34.662 --> 00:07:37.039
And then Jimmie said, "Dave, be nice."

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:38.291
So my mother says yes.

00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:40.126
I go to Jimmie's apartment.

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:42.336
I walk into Jimmie's apartment,

00:07:42.336 --> 00:07:44.172
and sitting there is Wayne Klein,

00:07:45.089 --> 00:07:47.717
Jimmie Walker, Jay Leno,

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:50.344
and David Letterman,

00:07:50.344 --> 00:07:51.345
and Marty Nadler,

00:07:51.345 --> 00:07:52.638
who went on to write and produce

00:07:52.638 --> 00:07:54.390
"Laverne and Shirley" and "Happy Days."

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:55.516
Wayne Klein went on to write...

00:07:55.516 --> 00:07:56.934
- All of these people in the same room?

00:07:56.934 --> 00:07:57.768
- Sitting in the room,

00:07:57.768 --> 00:07:59.312
sitting in Jimmie's apartment

00:07:59.312 --> 00:08:02.315
on Sunset, 1975.

00:08:02.315 --> 00:08:03.149
- Just so you know,

00:08:03.149 --> 00:08:04.400
everything that you just said

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.401
is not normal.

00:08:05.401 --> 00:08:07.153
I hope you know that.

00:08:07.153 --> 00:08:09.030
But name all these different people.

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:10.490
Right? All these different people.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:12.116
I'm sure you learned a ton of things,

00:08:12.116 --> 00:08:13.576
but what was one of the biggest things

00:08:13.576 --> 00:08:14.410
that you take away

00:08:14.410 --> 00:08:15.661
from even, like, Johnny Carson,

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:16.537
all these different people

00:08:16.537 --> 00:08:18.956
that you came across at a early age.

00:08:18.956 --> 00:08:20.458
- Love and passion.

00:08:20.458 --> 00:08:21.501
Love what you do

00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:23.002
and have enormous passion for it.

00:08:23.002 --> 00:08:24.921
I remember when I said, "Oh my God,

00:08:24.921 --> 00:08:26.172
I wanna be in television.

00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:27.507
I wanna write and produce."

00:08:27.507 --> 00:08:29.217
I said I will literally

00:08:29.217 --> 00:08:30.551
do this for free.

00:08:30.551 --> 00:08:32.220
I literally will do it for free.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:34.096
- That's how I felt about basketball.

00:08:34.096 --> 00:08:35.306
- When you feel that way.

00:08:35.306 --> 00:08:36.140
- Yeah.

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:37.266
- Then everything else is...

00:08:37.266 --> 00:08:39.143
The moment you find what you wanna do

00:08:39.143 --> 00:08:39.977
and you love it,

00:08:39.977 --> 00:08:41.187
is the moment in my mind

00:08:41.187 --> 00:08:42.730
you become successful.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:45.483
- Obviously the different things

00:08:45.483 --> 00:08:46.984
that an early age impacted you.

00:08:46.984 --> 00:08:48.277
But I think

00:08:48.277 --> 00:08:49.529
one of my biggest questions for you,

00:08:49.529 --> 00:08:52.657
because you were a writer, performer,

00:08:52.657 --> 00:08:54.659
when did it change for you

00:08:54.659 --> 00:08:57.203
and you said, the business side.

00:08:57.203 --> 00:08:58.162
- You know, I realized early on,

00:08:58.162 --> 00:09:00.748
it's not show business.

00:09:00.748 --> 00:09:01.916
It's business show.

00:09:02.792 --> 00:09:04.585
Juxtapose those two words,

00:09:04.585 --> 00:09:05.419
and now you can do

00:09:05.419 --> 00:09:07.421
all the shows you want.

00:09:07.421 --> 00:09:09.131
Learn the business side.

00:09:09.131 --> 00:09:10.216
A lot of people show up,

00:09:10.216 --> 00:09:12.093
they wanna be Elvis.

00:09:12.093 --> 00:09:13.803
Nah. You gotta show up,

00:09:13.803 --> 00:09:16.138
you gotta be John D. Rockefeller.

00:09:16.138 --> 00:09:17.807
- Were you learning that the whole time?

00:09:17.807 --> 00:09:18.641
- You know what happened?

00:09:18.641 --> 00:09:20.017
I was in a negotiation -

00:09:20.017 --> 00:09:21.811
and it didn't go well - on a show

00:09:21.811 --> 00:09:23.020
that I was hosting.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:24.981
And I didn't like the feeling,

00:09:24.981 --> 00:09:25.815
cause it was like

00:09:25.815 --> 00:09:26.649
they were determining

00:09:26.649 --> 00:09:27.483
my value, my worth.

00:09:27.483 --> 00:09:29.068
And I felt I was worth more.

00:09:29.068 --> 00:09:30.820
And I decided, "You know what?

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:32.321
I'ma learn the business."

00:09:32.321 --> 00:09:33.239
And so I went to

00:09:33.239 --> 00:09:34.657
my first television convention

00:09:34.657 --> 00:09:37.618
in January of '81.

00:09:37.618 --> 00:09:38.828
And in January of '81

00:09:38.828 --> 00:09:41.539
I was 19 years old.

00:09:41.539 --> 00:09:42.582
I developed some of

00:09:42.582 --> 00:09:44.041
my best relationships going,

00:09:44.041 --> 00:09:44.917
just showing up.

00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:46.836
And my mother would say to me, show up.

00:09:46.836 --> 00:09:47.670
- Yep.

00:09:47.670 --> 00:09:49.630
- That's that's like 80% of winning

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:50.881
is showing up.

00:09:50.881 --> 00:09:51.716
- You gotta give yourself a chance.

00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:52.883
- Gotta keep yourself a chance.

00:09:52.883 --> 00:09:53.718
I said, "All right."

00:09:53.718 --> 00:09:55.011
And I went and I showed up

00:09:55.011 --> 00:09:56.012
and I wouldn't leave.

00:09:56.012 --> 00:09:57.763
I wouldn't leave the lobby of the hotel

00:09:57.763 --> 00:09:59.015
until I met everybody.

00:09:59.015 --> 00:10:00.683
And I've gone to that convention

00:10:00.683 --> 00:10:04.437
for 40 consecutive years,

00:10:04.437 --> 00:10:06.480
40 consecutive years.

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:07.315
One of my buddies

00:10:07.315 --> 00:10:08.149
who I met at the convention,

00:10:08.149 --> 00:10:09.358
Dick Robertson -

00:10:09.358 --> 00:10:10.943
he ran Warner Brothers,

00:10:10.943 --> 00:10:13.070
Warner Brothers Television Distribution.

00:10:13.070 --> 00:10:15.114
Brought in a couple of billion a year.

00:10:15.114 --> 00:10:17.533
So I'm out at his beach house in Malibu.

00:10:17.533 --> 00:10:18.701
And we're out on his deck.

00:10:18.701 --> 00:10:19.869
We're looking out at the ocean

00:10:19.869 --> 00:10:21.162
drinking lemonade,

00:10:21.162 --> 00:10:22.663
and he's got a couple of Labs

00:10:22.663 --> 00:10:23.706
and I'm petting the Labs.

00:10:23.706 --> 00:10:25.750
And he just casually says to me,

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:27.335
"Yeah, you know that television show,

00:10:27.335 --> 00:10:29.670
that once-a-week, one-hour TV show

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:30.921
that we syndicate called

00:10:30.921 --> 00:10:32.256
"HBO Comedy Hour"?

00:10:32.256 --> 00:10:33.591
Where they would take comedians

00:10:33.591 --> 00:10:35.259
doing their specials.

00:10:35.259 --> 00:10:36.969
He goes, "I gave that show

00:10:36.969 --> 00:10:38.137
to a friend of ours."

00:10:38.137 --> 00:10:39.722
I go, "Why'd you give that show

00:10:39.722 --> 00:10:40.681
to a friend of yours?"

00:10:40.681 --> 00:10:42.058
He said,

00:10:42.058 --> 00:10:43.267
"Because that's a once-a-week show

00:10:43.267 --> 00:10:46.687
that only brings in 10 million a year."

00:10:46.687 --> 00:10:47.521
And he says,

00:10:47.521 --> 00:10:48.939
"And I have my salespeople

00:10:48.939 --> 00:10:50.274
going out on the road

00:10:50.274 --> 00:10:53.569
to do the first cycle of "Friends."

00:10:53.569 --> 00:10:54.820
And I expect them to come back

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.489
like with a billion dollars.

00:10:56.489 --> 00:10:57.823
And I don't want them" -

00:10:57.823 --> 00:10:59.158
he said, quote -

00:10:59.158 --> 00:11:01.661
"messing around with a once-a-week show

00:11:01.661 --> 00:11:04.872
that only brings in 10 million a year."

00:11:04.872 --> 00:11:06.248
- And you was like, hmm.

00:11:06.248 --> 00:11:09.251
- And I said, "Wow, OK."

00:11:09.251 --> 00:11:10.461
And I went home,

00:11:10.461 --> 00:11:11.295
and the next day

00:11:11.295 --> 00:11:12.296
I started my company

00:11:12.296 --> 00:11:13.297
from my dining room table

00:11:13.297 --> 00:11:15.591
selling a once-a-week one-hour show -

00:11:15.591 --> 00:11:17.927
chasing a once-a-week one-hour show

00:11:17.927 --> 00:11:19.887
that could bring in 10 million a year.

00:11:19.887 --> 00:11:20.721
And I was like,

00:11:20.721 --> 00:11:22.390
"I'ma take studio crumbs,

00:11:22.390 --> 00:11:24.141
and I'ma make a gourmet meal."

00:11:24.141 --> 00:11:25.184
(Byron laughing)

00:11:25.184 --> 00:11:26.018
- How has it been

00:11:26.018 --> 00:11:26.894
with family and friends

00:11:26.894 --> 00:11:27.728
over the years,

00:11:27.728 --> 00:11:28.729
like as far as support,

00:11:28.729 --> 00:11:31.357
going from one stage of your career

00:11:31.357 --> 00:11:32.733
to the next?

00:11:32.733 --> 00:11:33.859
- You know, it's so funny,

00:11:33.859 --> 00:11:34.902
Johnny Carson said,

00:11:34.902 --> 00:11:37.029
"You know what happens is, you know,

00:11:37.029 --> 00:11:38.948
when you start to have success,"

00:11:38.948 --> 00:11:41.158
he goes, "you don't change,

00:11:41.158 --> 00:11:43.160
the people around you change."

00:11:43.160 --> 00:11:45.454
And so I learned early on,

00:11:45.454 --> 00:11:46.622
OK I gotta go a little...

00:11:46.622 --> 00:11:47.581
I gotta go the extra mile

00:11:47.581 --> 00:11:48.624
make them comfortable.

00:11:48.624 --> 00:11:49.750
I gotta let my friends know

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:50.751
I love you, I appreciate you,

00:11:50.751 --> 00:11:52.128
nothing's changed.

00:11:52.128 --> 00:11:52.962
As a matter of fact,

00:11:52.962 --> 00:11:53.796
you coming with me,

00:11:53.796 --> 00:11:54.964
I'm not leaving you behind.

00:11:54.964 --> 00:11:56.424
You're part of this.

00:11:56.424 --> 00:11:58.676
You know, I have a family-owned company.

00:11:58.676 --> 00:11:59.677
My mother's office

00:11:59.677 --> 00:12:01.137
is next door to my office.

00:12:01.137 --> 00:12:02.596
You knock on the wall,

00:12:02.596 --> 00:12:04.140
you knocking on her wall, right?

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:06.559
So my uncle is my chief operating officer.

00:12:06.559 --> 00:12:08.185
People don't know he's my uncle,

00:12:08.185 --> 00:12:10.146
cause I don't normally broadcast that.

00:12:10.146 --> 00:12:12.690
So - I love my family,

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:14.191
and I've even said - right?

00:12:15.109 --> 00:12:17.403
I say to my kids, my three kids,

00:12:17.403 --> 00:12:18.237
I said, "You know, look,

00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:19.405
I need you to get that education,

00:12:19.405 --> 00:12:21.240
because I want you to come

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:22.283
hang with daddy.

00:12:22.283 --> 00:12:23.117
I want you to be here

00:12:23.117 --> 00:12:24.326
at the office with me.

00:12:24.326 --> 00:12:25.911
I want us to build legacy wealth.

00:12:25.911 --> 00:12:26.746
Now, just in case

00:12:26.746 --> 00:12:27.580
you're not feeling it,

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:28.372
then I get it.

00:12:28.372 --> 00:12:30.166
Then I'll just take the company public.

00:12:30.166 --> 00:12:31.000
And you know,

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:31.834
you guys sit on the board,

00:12:31.834 --> 00:12:33.085
if you don't wanna run it, whatever.

00:12:33.085 --> 00:12:35.379
But it's about building legacy wealth."

00:12:35.379 --> 00:12:36.589
- I just have to ask you,

00:12:36.589 --> 00:12:38.466
what is the most memorable thing

00:12:38.466 --> 00:12:39.675
in your business career?

00:12:39.675 --> 00:12:40.676
I'm sure there's a lot,

00:12:40.676 --> 00:12:41.510
but I'm just saying like,

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:43.804
what's one thing that you was like, wow,

00:12:43.804 --> 00:12:46.515
like I'm happy this happened.

00:12:46.515 --> 00:12:47.975
- The big moment for me

00:12:47.975 --> 00:12:49.518
was buying the Weather Channel.

00:12:50.519 --> 00:12:52.104
And the reason why that was a big moment

00:12:52.104 --> 00:12:53.731
is because quite often

00:12:53.731 --> 00:12:55.816
as African American entrepreneurs,

00:12:55.816 --> 00:12:57.401
we're in the negro leagues.

00:12:57.401 --> 00:12:58.235
And there's nothing wrong

00:12:58.235 --> 00:12:59.320
with the negro leagues,

00:12:59.320 --> 00:13:00.154
but the one thing

00:13:00.154 --> 00:13:01.280
I've always wanted to send

00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:03.824
to all these young Black kids: an image

00:13:03.824 --> 00:13:05.785
of the global leagues.

00:13:05.785 --> 00:13:07.119
We don't just do business

00:13:07.119 --> 00:13:08.454
in our neighborhood.

00:13:08.454 --> 00:13:10.498
We do business in every neighborhood.

00:13:10.498 --> 00:13:11.582
- That's real, that's real.

00:13:11.582 --> 00:13:12.416
When I saw that,

00:13:12.416 --> 00:13:14.543
I was like, so happy for you.

00:13:14.543 --> 00:13:16.003
You know, like that's a big deal.

00:13:16.003 --> 00:13:16.837
- Thank you.

00:13:16.837 --> 00:13:17.922
It's the first time

00:13:17.922 --> 00:13:19.465
an African American has owned

00:13:19.465 --> 00:13:21.967
a mainstream cable network.

00:13:21.967 --> 00:13:23.761
It was the No. 1 cable network

00:13:23.761 --> 00:13:24.929
last weekend.

00:13:24.929 --> 00:13:26.138
And so, when you think about

00:13:26.138 --> 00:13:28.599
what's owned by the big corporations,

00:13:28.599 --> 00:13:30.559
News Corp owns Fox news,

00:13:30.559 --> 00:13:32.520
Comcast own MSNBC,

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:34.814
and I own the Weather Channel.

00:13:34.814 --> 00:13:36.899
So that was sending a message

00:13:36.899 --> 00:13:38.150
to all those kids out there:

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:39.735
"I want you to see yourself

00:13:39.735 --> 00:13:40.778
where you're global.

00:13:40.778 --> 00:13:42.071
Just because you're Black,

00:13:42.071 --> 00:13:43.322
you're not gonna do business

00:13:43.322 --> 00:13:44.698
in just Black neighborhoods,

00:13:44.698 --> 00:13:45.950
and that's fine.

00:13:45.950 --> 00:13:47.535
But I also want you to know

00:13:47.535 --> 00:13:49.537
you are playing it globally."

00:13:49.537 --> 00:13:51.580
- Right, and you spoke about it

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:52.414
a second ago,

00:13:52.414 --> 00:13:53.749
when you talked about legacy, right?

00:13:53.749 --> 00:13:55.960
And historically in Hollywood,

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:59.380
it's a huge lack of diversity,

00:13:59.380 --> 00:14:00.214
right?
- Yeah.

00:14:00.214 --> 00:14:01.757
- So what type of solutions

00:14:01.757 --> 00:14:03.926
do you see that could change that?

00:14:03.926 --> 00:14:05.803
- Don't play the game, own the game.

00:14:05.803 --> 00:14:07.680
I've always been about owning the game.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:09.640
When I met my wife 20 years ago,

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:10.432
she's like, "What are you doing?"

00:14:10.432 --> 00:14:12.309
I was working from my dining room table.

00:14:12.309 --> 00:14:13.143
I said, "I'm building

00:14:13.143 --> 00:14:14.728
the world's biggest media company."

00:14:14.728 --> 00:14:16.105
And I've never deviated

00:14:16.105 --> 00:14:17.147
from that vision.

00:14:17.147 --> 00:14:18.190
And as I build it,

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:20.192
I'm going to effectuate change

00:14:20.192 --> 00:14:22.069
for the greater good for the planet,

00:14:22.069 --> 00:14:23.529
because there are media companies

00:14:23.529 --> 00:14:25.281
out there that divide us.

00:14:25.281 --> 00:14:26.615
I'm gonna unite us.

00:14:26.615 --> 00:14:28.659
Look, the president of the United States

00:14:28.659 --> 00:14:29.535
is not the most powerful person

00:14:29.535 --> 00:14:30.411
in the world.

00:14:30.411 --> 00:14:31.829
The most powerful person in the world

00:14:31.829 --> 00:14:33.706
is the person who controls media,

00:14:33.706 --> 00:14:35.332
because we control what you hear,

00:14:35.332 --> 00:14:36.709
what you see, what you think,

00:14:36.709 --> 00:14:37.543
what you read,

00:14:37.543 --> 00:14:38.961
and ultimately how you behave.

00:14:38.961 --> 00:14:39.795
- Absolutely.

00:14:39.795 --> 00:14:40.629
- So that's a very,

00:14:40.629 --> 00:14:41.922
very powerful position.

00:14:41.922 --> 00:14:43.340
And you have to manage it

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:44.633
in a positive way

00:14:44.633 --> 00:14:47.386
and do things to make the world better.

00:14:47.386 --> 00:14:49.096
- But the reason why you're so important

00:14:49.096 --> 00:14:50.681
to all of us,

00:14:50.681 --> 00:14:52.683
and especially someone like me -

00:14:52.683 --> 00:14:55.311
obviously I grew up playing basketball.

00:14:55.311 --> 00:14:56.478
I was in my backyard

00:14:56.478 --> 00:14:57.521
with my brother,

00:14:57.521 --> 00:14:59.440
just trying to be like Mike, right?

00:14:59.440 --> 00:15:00.858
Like, I wanted to be like Michael Jordan.

00:15:00.858 --> 00:15:02.276
And when you get to the NBA,

00:15:03.110 --> 00:15:05.446
you learn that, like you said,

00:15:05.446 --> 00:15:06.447
it is basketball,

00:15:06.447 --> 00:15:07.531
but it's also a business.

00:15:07.531 --> 00:15:08.365
- That's right.

00:15:08.365 --> 00:15:09.199
- You wanna also learn the business.

00:15:09.199 --> 00:15:11.243
So everything that you were saying

00:15:11.243 --> 00:15:12.703
just now about a media company,

00:15:12.703 --> 00:15:13.662
obviously I started

00:15:13.662 --> 00:15:16.498
a production company called Ohh DIP!!!

00:15:16.498 --> 00:15:19.752
And then with a few other athletes

00:15:19.752 --> 00:15:20.794
and guys in the league,

00:15:20.794 --> 00:15:22.671
we started another media entity

00:15:22.671 --> 00:15:23.714
called Players TV,

00:15:23.714 --> 00:15:25.925
which is about including athletes.

00:15:25.925 --> 00:15:26.759
- That's right.

00:15:26.759 --> 00:15:27.593
- Right, having athletes

00:15:27.593 --> 00:15:28.427
be a part of something

00:15:28.427 --> 00:15:29.386
bigger than you.

00:15:29.386 --> 00:15:31.221
And I think to hear you say everything

00:15:31.221 --> 00:15:32.932
that you said is,

00:15:32.932 --> 00:15:35.184
in anything you do, you want ownership.

00:15:35.184 --> 00:15:36.018
- You wanna own it

00:15:36.018 --> 00:15:37.853
and you wanna have a seat at the table.

00:15:37.853 --> 00:15:39.146
Because if you don't have a seat

00:15:39.146 --> 00:15:39.980
at the table

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:40.940
and you're not a part of the -

00:15:40.940 --> 00:15:42.983
and your voices aren't being heard

00:15:42.983 --> 00:15:44.193
and amplified -

00:15:44.193 --> 00:15:46.820
then it's not a real democracy.

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:47.988
We have a bunch of white guys

00:15:47.988 --> 00:15:49.365
who own Spanish-language networks

00:15:49.365 --> 00:15:50.866
and they can't even speak Spanish.

00:15:50.866 --> 00:15:51.951
People in the gay community

00:15:51.951 --> 00:15:52.993
don't own their networks.

00:15:52.993 --> 00:15:54.453
People from the Asian community,

00:15:54.453 --> 00:15:55.287
it's like,

00:15:55.287 --> 00:15:56.121
they're invisible.

00:15:56.121 --> 00:15:57.623
You don't even acknowledge them.

00:15:57.623 --> 00:15:58.707
Women really don't even

00:15:58.707 --> 00:16:00.042
own their networks.

00:16:00.042 --> 00:16:01.043
My position is,

00:16:01.043 --> 00:16:03.754
everybody needs to own these networks

00:16:03.754 --> 00:16:05.089
and own this content.

00:16:05.089 --> 00:16:06.548
And you need to amplify

00:16:06.548 --> 00:16:07.967
and produce yourself.

00:16:07.967 --> 00:16:09.218
I'm about the four E's.

00:16:09.218 --> 00:16:10.052
Make sure everybody

00:16:10.052 --> 00:16:11.470
has a great education.

00:16:11.470 --> 00:16:13.847
Make sure everybody has equal justice.

00:16:13.847 --> 00:16:14.765
Make sure everybody

00:16:14.765 --> 00:16:16.517
has economic inclusion.

00:16:16.517 --> 00:16:17.351
And make sure

00:16:17.351 --> 00:16:19.311
everybody has environmental protection.

00:16:19.311 --> 00:16:20.229
And you get those four E's,

00:16:20.229 --> 00:16:21.897
that equals the fifth E,

00:16:21.897 --> 00:16:23.065
which is excellence.

00:16:23.065 --> 00:16:25.359
If you're successful I'm successful,

00:16:25.359 --> 00:16:27.194
your success is my success.

00:16:27.194 --> 00:16:28.696
My success is your success.

00:16:28.696 --> 00:16:29.613
- How do you know

00:16:29.613 --> 00:16:31.115
what business risk to take?

00:16:32.408 --> 00:16:34.076
- I'm comfortable with business risk.

00:16:34.076 --> 00:16:35.452
I mean, I'm a big believer

00:16:35.452 --> 00:16:36.954
in swinging for the fences.

00:16:36.954 --> 00:16:39.748
I mean, my first movie that I put out,

00:16:39.748 --> 00:16:40.916
I went to a buddy of mine,

00:16:40.916 --> 00:16:42.167
and I said, "You know what?

00:16:42.167 --> 00:16:43.961
I got an opp - I found a shark movie

00:16:43.961 --> 00:16:45.045
that we could put out."

00:16:45.045 --> 00:16:47.006
And I said, "I need $30 million."

00:16:47.006 --> 00:16:48.424
I said, "I'll put up 15 million,

00:16:48.424 --> 00:16:49.425
you put up 15 million,

00:16:49.425 --> 00:16:51.218
but I need you to understand something.

00:16:51.218 --> 00:16:52.052
You're probably

00:16:52.052 --> 00:16:53.721
gonna lose your 15 million.

00:16:53.721 --> 00:16:55.681
You're gonna lose every last penny

00:16:55.681 --> 00:16:56.640
of this money,

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.434
but I'm gonna lose my 15 million

00:16:58.434 --> 00:16:59.727
next to yours."

00:16:59.727 --> 00:17:01.228
And my friend said to me,

00:17:01.228 --> 00:17:02.813
"Is that your pitch?"

00:17:02.813 --> 00:17:03.856
I go yeah. I said

00:17:03.856 --> 00:17:04.815
"I'm just telling you the truth.

00:17:04.815 --> 00:17:05.649
That's my pitch."

00:17:05.649 --> 00:17:07.484
He goes, "That is the worst pitch

00:17:07.484 --> 00:17:09.737
I have ever heard in my life."

00:17:09.737 --> 00:17:12.072
He goes, "You got my 15 million."

00:17:12.072 --> 00:17:13.699
So he put down 15 million.

00:17:13.699 --> 00:17:14.908
I put down 15 million.

00:17:14.908 --> 00:17:16.368
We put out this shark movie,

00:17:16.368 --> 00:17:17.244
ended up being the biggest

00:17:17.244 --> 00:17:18.495
independent movie of the summer.

00:17:18.495 --> 00:17:19.705
We did over 44 million

00:17:19.705 --> 00:17:20.873
at the box office.

00:17:20.873 --> 00:17:21.707
And we still laugh about it.

00:17:21.707 --> 00:17:22.541
- That's a hell of a story.

00:17:22.541 --> 00:17:23.959
(Byron laughs)

00:17:23.959 --> 00:17:24.793
- He said,

00:17:24.793 --> 00:17:25.836
"Everybody who's ever come to me

00:17:25.836 --> 00:17:26.795
and pitched - they always said

00:17:26.795 --> 00:17:27.880
I was gonna make a bunch of money.

00:17:27.880 --> 00:17:29.214
You just told me the truth

00:17:29.214 --> 00:17:30.716
and said I was gonna lose it all.

00:17:30.716 --> 00:17:32.134
And here we made all this money."

00:17:32.134 --> 00:17:34.386
I go, "I honestly could not tell you

00:17:34.386 --> 00:17:35.387
we're gonna have one person

00:17:35.387 --> 00:17:36.388
buy one ticket,

00:17:36.388 --> 00:17:37.347
but I could tell you,

00:17:37.347 --> 00:17:38.223
I would lose money

00:17:38.223 --> 00:17:39.558
side by side with you."

00:17:39.558 --> 00:17:41.310
- That's a heck of a story.

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:42.644
And I'll tell you this.

00:17:43.645 --> 00:17:45.314
I mean learning everything

00:17:45.314 --> 00:17:46.982
has been unbelievably amazing.

00:17:46.982 --> 00:17:49.109
But one question I always like to ask.

00:17:49.109 --> 00:17:49.943
- Sure.

00:17:49.943 --> 00:17:50.778
- This may be

00:17:50.778 --> 00:17:52.863
just a personal aspect for me,

00:17:52.863 --> 00:17:54.239
but anytime I find out

00:17:54.239 --> 00:17:55.949
someone has kids, right?

00:17:55.949 --> 00:17:57.159
- Yeah.

00:17:57.159 --> 00:17:58.285
- For me, cause I don't have

00:17:58.285 --> 00:17:59.119
all the answers.

00:17:59.119 --> 00:17:59.953
I always say,

00:17:59.953 --> 00:18:01.914
if someone had a book on raising kids,

00:18:01.914 --> 00:18:02.748
then they're lying.

00:18:02.748 --> 00:18:03.582
- That's right.

00:18:03.582 --> 00:18:04.458
- Cause everybody is different.

00:18:04.458 --> 00:18:05.292
- That's right.

00:18:05.292 --> 00:18:06.126
- But one of the things

00:18:06.126 --> 00:18:08.796
that I've sort of seen

00:18:08.796 --> 00:18:10.464
or always noticed is that

00:18:10.464 --> 00:18:12.216
I feel like your kids will be raised

00:18:12.216 --> 00:18:14.051
with a different level of privilege

00:18:14.051 --> 00:18:15.385
than maybe you did.

00:18:15.385 --> 00:18:16.220
- That's right.

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:17.554
- So being the way

00:18:17.554 --> 00:18:19.556
that you were brought up,

00:18:19.556 --> 00:18:22.101
you know, in a very moderate family -

00:18:22.101 --> 00:18:23.435
I know I was, too.

00:18:23.435 --> 00:18:24.478
How do you make sure that

00:18:24.478 --> 00:18:26.021
you keep your kids grounded

00:18:26.021 --> 00:18:27.189
and at the same time

00:18:27.189 --> 00:18:28.273
keep 'em motivated?

00:18:28.273 --> 00:18:30.192
- Oh, I'm good at reminding them,

00:18:30.192 --> 00:18:31.401
"This is my money not yours."

00:18:31.401 --> 00:18:32.236
(Byron laughs)

00:18:32.236 --> 00:18:33.278
- Right, right, yeah.

00:18:33.278 --> 00:18:34.947
- So I want you to know,

00:18:34.947 --> 00:18:35.948
don't get too comfortable.

00:18:35.948 --> 00:18:36.782
- Exactly.

00:18:36.782 --> 00:18:37.699
- You know, so,

00:18:37.699 --> 00:18:41.703
my kids, they know they are privileged

00:18:41.703 --> 00:18:42.621
and they're blessed,

00:18:42.621 --> 00:18:43.914
but they're not spoiled.

00:18:43.914 --> 00:18:44.748
And that is,

00:18:44.748 --> 00:18:45.582
you gotta work that.

00:18:45.582 --> 00:18:46.416
But the main thing is

00:18:46.416 --> 00:18:47.751
I wanted my kids to be happy,

00:18:47.751 --> 00:18:49.878
healthy and have empathy.

00:18:49.878 --> 00:18:52.005
And my wife, credit goes to her.

00:18:52.005 --> 00:18:54.508
She does an amazing job with our kids.

00:18:54.508 --> 00:18:56.593
I would take our kids to school -

00:18:56.593 --> 00:18:57.427
I remember taking

00:18:57.427 --> 00:18:58.762
our 3-year-old daughter to school.

00:18:58.762 --> 00:19:00.055
And I remember her telling us,

00:19:00.055 --> 00:19:00.973
saying to me, "Daddy,

00:19:00.973 --> 00:19:02.099
I see another homeless person.

00:19:02.099 --> 00:19:03.684
Daddy, I see another homeless person."

00:19:03.684 --> 00:19:04.518
She said, "Daddy,

00:19:04.518 --> 00:19:06.353
why aren't we stopping to help them?"

00:19:06.353 --> 00:19:08.230
And I started crying

00:19:08.230 --> 00:19:10.274
because I realized -

00:19:10.274 --> 00:19:11.191
what was it about me

00:19:11.191 --> 00:19:13.152
that I turned off my empathy button?

00:19:13.152 --> 00:19:13.986
And acting like

00:19:13.986 --> 00:19:14.820
I didn't see that.

00:19:14.820 --> 00:19:15.654
And it took this little

00:19:15.654 --> 00:19:16.488
3-year-old girl

00:19:16.488 --> 00:19:17.322
to turn it back on.

00:19:17.322 --> 00:19:18.490
I said, "You're right.

00:19:18.490 --> 00:19:19.575
And we are gonna help 'em."

00:19:19.575 --> 00:19:20.909
But they want to help.

00:19:20.909 --> 00:19:22.411
They want to lean in.

00:19:22.411 --> 00:19:23.579
And I love that.

00:19:23.579 --> 00:19:25.956
They have that compassion

00:19:25.956 --> 00:19:27.499
and that is so important.

00:19:27.499 --> 00:19:29.459
Everything else we can help with.

00:19:29.459 --> 00:19:30.460
We'll make sure

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:31.295
you go to the best schools.

00:19:31.295 --> 00:19:32.671
We'll make sure you're educated

00:19:32.671 --> 00:19:35.007
but more importantly, intelligent.

00:19:35.007 --> 00:19:35.841
There are a lot of people

00:19:35.841 --> 00:19:36.675
who are educated,

00:19:36.675 --> 00:19:38.135
but they're not that intelligent.

00:19:38.135 --> 00:19:39.720
So we'll make sure

00:19:39.720 --> 00:19:42.347
you're gonna be a great contributor

00:19:42.347 --> 00:19:43.515
to this world.

00:19:43.515 --> 00:19:44.349
That's all I want.

00:19:44.349 --> 00:19:45.934
- Yeah that's the biggest job

00:19:45.934 --> 00:19:47.728
that we have.

00:19:47.728 --> 00:19:49.563
That's that bigger than any shot

00:19:49.563 --> 00:19:50.564
that I could ever make

00:19:50.564 --> 00:19:52.065
in a game where I could win.

00:19:52.065 --> 00:19:53.525
And I bet for any business deal

00:19:53.525 --> 00:19:54.526
that you could ever do.

00:19:54.526 --> 00:19:55.944
- And that's how I'm playing.

00:19:55.944 --> 00:19:58.989
- So listen, I cannot

00:19:58.989 --> 00:20:00.199
say thank you enough

00:20:00.199 --> 00:20:01.533
for all the knowledge

00:20:01.533 --> 00:20:02.451
that you just shared with me.

00:20:02.451 --> 00:20:03.368
I appreciate you.

00:20:03.368 --> 00:20:04.203
- You got it.

00:20:04.203 --> 00:20:05.037
- All right.

00:20:05.037 --> 00:20:05.871
- Thank you, baby.

00:20:05.871 --> 00:20:07.289
(Both laughing)

